      "latency_ms": 4849.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 25,
      "completion_tokens": 826,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and colorless liquid that is essential for all known forms of life. It is composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, chemically represented as **H₂O**. Here’s a breakdown of its key characteristics:\n\n1. **Chemical Structure**:  \n   Each water molecule consists of one oxygen atom bonded to two hydrogen atoms. The molecule is **polar**, meaning it has a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a slight negative charge on the oxygen side. This polarity allows water to dissolve many substances, earning it the title of \"universal solvent.\"\n\n2. **Physical States**:  \n   Water exists in three states:  \n   - **Solid** (ice): Forms when water freezes at 0°C (32°F).  \n   - **Liquid**: The most common state at room temperature.  \n   - **Gas** (water vapor): Produced when water boils at 100°C (212°F) or evaporates at lower temperatures.  \n\n3. **Unique Properties**:  \n   - **Hydrogen Bonding**: Water molecules stick together due to hydrogen bonds, giving it high **surface tension**, **specific heat capacity** (it absorbs and releases heat slowly), and **cohesion** (molecules stick to each other).  \n   - **Density Anomaly**: Ice is less dense than liquid water, allowing it to float. This is critical for aquatic life in cold climates.  \n\n4. **Role in Life**:  \n   Water is vital for biological processes:  \n   - It acts as a **solvent** for nutrients and waste in cells.  \n   - It regulates body temperature through sweating and respiration.  \n   - It is a key component of blood, cells, and tissues.  \n\n5. **Abundance and Distribution**:  \n   Water covers about 71% of Earth’s surface, but only ~2.5% is freshwater, with most locked in ice caps and glaciers. The rest is found in rivers, lakes, and groundwater.  \n\nIn summary, water is a fundamental substance with unique physical and chemical properties that make it indispensable for life, ecosystems, and Earth’s natural cycles."
